// Jurisdictional messaging rules — message class x jurisdiction, as data.
//
// Counsel round 26a. The rules are data because they are not one rule: what a
// message owes depends on WHAT it is and WHERE the recipient is, and code that
// answers that with an `if` grows a global rule out of whichever jurisdiction
// its author happened to study.
//
// ── SIX RULES FOR THIS FILE ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// 1. NO RULE WITHOUT A CITATION. Every requirement names at least one entry in
//    `citations`, and every citation carries `authority`, `checked_on`,
//    `verified_against` and — unless it is `unverified` — the `quote` that was
//    actually read. This is not ceremony. Two claims in this repository were
//    already wrong in exactly the way an uncited rule is wrong: a TCPA
//    quiet-hours range asserted wider than the regulation's own scope, and
//    Washington recorded as carrying a signature exemption it does not have.
//    Both came from second-hand summaries that transplanted a neighbouring
//    rule. A quote from the publisher's own text is the only thing that stops
//    it.
//
//    And ONE of those citations is named as the `basis_citation` — the provision
//    the value actually rests on. "At least one of my four citations is primary
//    text" was the first version of that check and it was not enough: it passed
//    a rule whose own exception provision had been downgraded to a summary,
//    because a sibling citation was still primary. Proven by mutating this file.
//
// 2. `verified_against` SAYS WHERE THE WORDS CAME FROM, on this ladder:
//      primary_text        — the publisher's own text (govinfo, laws-lois.justice.gc.ca)
//      primary_text_mirror — a law library's verbatim reproduction, quoted here
//      secondary_source    — a memo, guidance summary or article
//      unverified          — NOBODY HAS READ IT. The authority is named so the
//                            next person knows what to open; the claim is not
//                            settled and must not be read as if it were.
//    The gate COUNTS unverified citations and prints the count on every run. An
//    unverified statutory claim that reads as verified is worse than an absent
//    one, so it is never allowed to hide inside a green run.
//
// 3. "A STATUTE EXEMPTS US" AND "OUR ARCHITECTURE DOES NOT TRIGGER IT" ARE
//    DIFFERENT FACTS. This repository has conflated them before. So the legal
//    reason lives in `basis_kind` and the engineering reason lives in
//    `architecture_note`, and they are separate fields:
//      statute_requires    — the provision affirmatively imposes this
//      statute_scope       — the provision's own definitions do not reach this
//                            case (cite the DEFINITION, not the duty)
//      statutory_exception — the provision reaches it and then excepts it
//                            (cite the exception; primary text only)
//      architecture        — the provision would reach it; our design never
//                            performs the triggering act (requires
//                            `architecture_note`)
//      product_posture     — no statute demands this; we do it anyway
//      unknown             — we have not established the answer
//    An `architecture_note` may never be the support for a legal conclusion,
//    and `statutory_exception` may never rest on a summary. The gate enforces
//    both.
//
// 4. `unknown` IS A LEGAL VALUE AND FAILS CLOSED. Where we have not read the
//    authority, the requirement says `unknown`, names the authority as an
//    unverified citation, and states the open question. The send gate refuses
//    rather than proceeding. Guessing `not_applicable` is how an unstudied
//    jurisdiction becomes a silent green light.
//
// 5. NO JURISDICTION FALLBACK, IN THIS FILE OR IN THE ACCESSOR. A rule answers
//    for the exact country/region pair it declares. `rulesFor` throws for
//    anything else. A fallback is precisely how a rule proven in one
//    jurisdiction becomes a global rule, which is the thing this framework
//    exists to prevent — and the reason there is no `US`-with-no-region entry
//    even though the federal provisions cited below apply nationally: adding a
//    state means studying that state's overlay, not inheriting California's.
//
// 6. `CA` IS AMBIGUOUS AND THIS FILE NEVER LEANS ON IT. `{country: "US",
//    region: "CA"}` is California; `{country: "CA", region: null}` is Canada.
//    Both appear below. Every entry states country and region as separate
//    fields, and the gate refuses a region of `"*"` or `"ALL"` so nobody can
//    make one entry answer for a country it has not studied.
//
// ── WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT HERE ───────────────────────────────────────────
// A blanket 8am-9pm rule for all SMS. Counsel round 26a-1 refused that premise:
// the restriction sits in the telephone-solicitation rules and a transactional
// robotext does not inherit it. And no area-code-derived timezone: a number
// ports and its holder travels, so an area code is not the recipient's local
// time. The evidence order counsel set is explicit recipient timezone ->
// verified address -> other reliable signal -> conservative fallback, and we
// hold none of the first three today, which is why a `required` quiet-hours
// rule refuses instead of computing a window.
{
  "reviewed_on": "2026-08-17",
  "authority": "docs/legal/2026-08-15-counsel-round-26-response.md (26a-1, 26a-2, 26a-5)",

  "rules": [
